= 1
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

In the position representation the <time-dependent Schrödinger equation> is
$$
\boxed{i\hbar\partial_t\psi=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\partial_x^2\psi+V(x)\psi.}
$$
For a normalized state define <probability density> and <probability current> by
$$
\rho=|\psi|^2,\qquad
j=\frac{\hbar}{2mi}(\overline\psi\psi_x-\psi\overline{\psi_x})
=\frac\hbar m\operatorname{Im}(\overline\psi\psi_x).
$$
Multiplying the equation by $\overline\psi$ and its <complex conjugate> by $\psi$, then subtracting, cancels the real potential. The resulting identity is
$$
\partial_t|\psi|^2=\frac{i\hbar}{2m}
(\overline\psi\psi_{xx}-\psi\overline{\psi_{xx}})=-\partial_xj.
$$
Thus $\boxed{\partial_t\rho+\partial_xj=0}$. Integrating over an interval gives $d\int_b^c\rho\,dx/dt=j(b)-j(c)$: probability changes only through flux across the endpoints. This is local <conservation of quantum probability>.

Continuity with the zero exterior imposes zero endpoint values on the <infinite square well>. Solving the stationary equation with these <boundary conditions> gives
$$
\boxed{E_n=\frac{n^2\pi^2\hbar^2}{2ma^2},\qquad
\Psi_n(x)=\sqrt{\frac2a}\sin\frac{n\pi x}a,\quad n\geq1,}
$$
with zero extension outside. The <integral> of $\sin^2(n\pi x/a)$ is $a/2$, which fixes the <normalization>. The initial coefficients are $3/5$ and $4/5$, whose squared moduli sum to one. Each energy component acquires its stationary phase:
$$
\boxed{\psi(x,t)=\frac35\Psi_1(x)e^{-iE_1t/\hbar}
+\frac45\Psi_2(x)e^{-iE_2t/\hbar}.}
$$
At the midpoint, $\Psi_1=\sqrt{2/a}$, $\Psi_1'=0$, $\Psi_2=0$, $\Psi_2'=-(2\pi/a)\sqrt{2/a}$. Hence
$$
\overline\psi\psi_x=-\frac{48\pi}{25a^2}e^{-i(E_2-E_1)t/\hbar}.
$$
Taking its imaginary part proves
$$
\boxed{j(a/2,t)=\frac{48\pi\hbar}{25ma^2}
\sin\left(\frac{3\pi^2\hbar}{2ma^2}t\right),\qquad
A=\frac{48\pi\hbar}{25ma^2},\quad\omega=\frac{3\pi^2\hbar}{2ma^2}.}
$$

= 2
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

For bound <hydrogen atom> states, the radial <wave function> is regular and finite at the origin, and decays at infinity so that $4\pi\int_0^\infty r^2|\Psi(r)|^2\,dr<\infty$. Equivalently the reduced radial function $w(r)=r\Psi(r)$ has $w(0)=0$ and is square integrable on the half-line. A singular $1/r$ branch is not an admissible regular Coulomb <eigenfunction>; its <derivative> energy diverges and it introduces an origin singularity absent from the <Hamiltonian> domain.

Write $\kappa=e^2/(4\pi\epsilon_0)$, so $a=\hbar^2/(m\kappa)$. Multiplication by $r$ turns the radial equation into
$$
w''+\left(-\mu^2+\frac2{ar}\right)w=0,qquad
\mu^2=-2mE/\hbar^2.
$$
Since $w=a e^{-\lambda R}f(R)$ and $r=aR$, substitution gives
$$
\boxed{f''-2\lambda f'+(\lambda^2-\mu^2a^2+2/R)f=0.}
$$
Choose $\boxed{\lambda=\mu a>0}$ to remove the constant coefficient and factor out the decaying exponential. For $f=\sum_{k\geq0}a_kR^{k+c}$, the lowest power has coefficient $c(c-1)a_0$, so $\boxed{c(c-1)=0}$. The $c=0$ behavior would give $\Psi\sim a_0/R$, violating regularity; choose $c=1$.

Matching successive powers then yields
$$
\boxed{a_{k+1}=\frac{2\{\lambda(k+1)-1\}}{(k+2)(k+1)}a_k.}
$$
A nonterminating tail eventually has one sign, and its coefficient ratio is asymptotic to $2\lambda/k$. More precisely, for any $0<\delta<\lambda$, the ratio is eventually at least $(2\lambda-\delta)/(k+1)$. Its same-sign tail consequently dominates a positive constant times the exponential series of rate $2\lambda-\delta$, up to finitely many terms. The factor $e^{-\lambda R}$ cannot then make $\Psi$ decay. Thus square integrability forces termination.

If the last nonzero term is $a_{n-1}R^n$, termination requires $\lambda n=1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{E_n=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2ma^2n^2}=-\frac A{2n^2},\qquad
A=\frac{\hbar^2}{ma^2}=\frac{me^4}{16\pi^2\epsilon_0^2\hbar^2}.}
$$
For $n=1$, $\lambda=1$, $f$ is proportional to $R$, and
$$
\boxed{\Psi_0(r)\propto e^{-r/a}.}
$$
The radial volume element is $4\pi r^2dr$, so the mean radius is the ratio of the radius-weighted <norm> <integral> to the <norm> <integral>. With $r=aR$, the common angular and <normalization> factors cancel:
$$
\langle r\rangle=a\frac{\int_0^\infty R^3e^{-2\lambda R}\,dR}
{\int_0^\infty R^2e^{-2\lambda R}\,dR}
=\frac{3a}{2\lambda}.
$$
In the <ground state> $\boxed{\langle r\rangle=3a/2}$.

= 3
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

For a normalized state and a <self-adjoint operator>, the <expectation value> and dispersion are
$$
\boxed{\langle A\rangle_\psi=\langle\psi|A\psi\rangle,\qquad
\Delta_\psi(A)=\sqrt{\langle A^2\rangle_\psi-\langle A\rangle_\psi^2}.}
$$
Use the physics convention that the <inner product> is conjugate-linear in its first argument, and assume the displayed products and second moments are defined. Self-adjointness gives $\overline{\langle\psi|A\psi\rangle}=\langle A\psi|\psi\rangle=\langle\psi|A\psi\rangle$, so the expectation is real. Moreover
$$
\Delta_\psi(A)^2=\|(A-\langle A\rangle_\psi)\psi\|^2.
$$
It vanishes exactly when $\psi$ is an eigenstate, with <eigenvalue> its expectation.

The product rule gives $XPf=-i\hbar xf'$ and $PXf=-i\hbar(f+xf')$, hence $\boxed{[X,P]f=i\hbar f}$ on their common differentiable domain. For general operators, put $z=\langle A\psi|B\psi\rangle$. Then
$$
\langle[A,B]\rangle=z-\overline z=2i\operatorname{Im}z=i\langle C\rangle.
$$
The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> yields
$$
\boxed{\langle A^2\rangle\langle B^2\rangle
\geq|z|^2\geq(\operatorname{Im}z)^2=\frac14\langle C\rangle^2.}
$$
Apply this to centered position and <momentum operators>, whose <commutator> is unchanged. Their squared <norms> are the <variances>, giving
$$
\boxed{\Delta P\,\Delta X\geq\hbar/2.}
$$
Equality requires both proportionality in Cauchy-Schwarz and a purely imaginary <inner product>. Thus for real means $x_0,p_0$,
$$
(P-p_0)\psi=i\gamma(X-x_0)\psi
$$
with real $\gamma$. Solving this first-order equation gives
$$
\psi(x)=N\exp\left[-\frac{\gamma(x-x_0)^2}{2\hbar}+\frac{ip_0x}{\hbar}\right].
$$
Normalizability on the real line forces $\gamma>0$. Writing $\sigma^2=\hbar/(2\gamma)$, all normalized equality states are therefore
$$
\boxed{\psi(x)=\frac{e^{i\phi}}{(2\pi\sigma^2)^{1/4}}
\exp\left[-\frac{(x-x_0)^2}{4\sigma^2}+\frac{ip_0x}{\hbar}\right],
\quad x_0,p_0,\phi\in\mathbb R,\quad\sigma>0.}
$$
They have $\Delta X=\sigma$ and $\Delta P=\hbar/(2\sigma)$. A nonconstant quadratic phase would increase the product, so the full family here has a real positive Gaussian width and only a linear spatial phase.

